+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- [ ] **`tests/test_bridge_run_driver.py`** (module docstring names R-039; every test name or docstring names R-039). Round 2's tests stay and pass, changed only as listed. Every launcher run uses a temporary launch folder, and every refusal test asserts `returncode == 125`, the one refusal line, `runs_of(folder) == []`, and runs with and without `BRIDGE_RUN_ALLOW_ANY=1`.
+  - **`test_r039_refuse_alternate_data_stream`, rewritten.** It creates `run/supervisor/x.txt` and then the stream: on Windows `open(str(base) + ":hidden.py", "w")`, on other platforms a regular file whose name is literally `x.txt:hidden.py` in `run/supervisor/`. The stream (or file) holds a program that writes a marker file under `tmp_path`. The test first asserts `os.path.isfile(<the stream's or file's full path>) is True`, then launches `run/supervisor/x.txt:hidden.py` and asserts exit 125, the refusal line, `runs_of(folder) == []` and that the marker file does not exist. On Windows only, if creating the stream raises `OSError`, the test calls `pytest.skip("the temporary folder's file system has no alternate data streams")`; your report says whether it ran or skipped.
+  - **`test_r039_refuse_dotdot_through_supervisor_link`, tightened.** It also creates `run/supervisor/x.py`, a program that writes a second marker file, so that `run/supervisor/j/../x.py` names an existing regular file once `..` collapses and only the `..` rule refuses it. Both marker files are absent.
